WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.432
Combining patterns, and choosing among them.

2
00:00:02.712 --> 00:00:04.952
Real features compose these patterns.

3
00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:20.997
The lab for this week does: a router picks the audience (exec, team, or client) and with it the model and format; a chain extracts facts, gates them, and drafts; an evaluator-optimizer loop polishes the draft against audience-specific checks.

4
00:00:21.277 --> 00:00:25.075
Each piece is still a workflow, because your code chooses every step.

5
00:00:25.355 --> 00:00:32.373
That is what keeps it testable: you will write offline tests for every branch with a fake client, before spending a cent.

6
00:00:32.653 --> 00:00:39.224
When choosing, ask the five questions from the start of the lesson in order, and stop at the first pattern that fits.

7
00:00:39.504 --> 00:00:41.168
Fixed steps suggest a chain.

8
00:00:41.448 --> 00:00:43.688
Distinct categories suggest a router.

9
00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:48.128
Independent parts or a need for confidence suggest parallelization.

10
00:00:48.408 --> 00:00:52.717
Input-dependent parts suggest orchestrator-workers, with its extra cost.

11
00:00:52.997 --> 00:00:58.501
Clear criteria and measurable gains from feedback suggest evaluator-optimizer.

12
00:00:58.781 --> 00:01:05.373
And if a single well-prompted call with the right context already meets your bar on an eval, none of them is needed.

13
00:01:05.653 --> 00:01:09.387
Lesson 3 makes that last option, and its reasons, concrete.
